INT. DROWNED BELL TOWER - BENEATH BLACKMERE LAKE - NIGHT

Air pocket in the belfry. Green light. Water slapping the stairs.

ORIN VESK, 60, salt-white beard, saws at the bell rope with a fishing knife. KESTREL AMBROSE, 20s, bandaged hands, hauls a sack of silver up the last dry step, TUPPENNY, 9, barefoot and bone-pale, hugs the bronze clapper and hums, and the stair they stood on a breath ago slides under the black water.

KESTREL
Leave the silver, Orin.

ORIN
Leave it and she follows us up.

TUPPENNY
She follows us anyway.

LAKE-WIFE (O.S.)
Give me back my bell, little thief.

KESTREL
Sweetest voice I ever heard for a drowning.

ORIN
Don't answer her.

KESTREL
I wasn't going to.

ORIN
You were.

ORIN
Cut the rope!

KESTREL ^
Ring the bell!

The bell booms once. The water shudders, flattens, goes glass.

Orin drops the knife. Kestrel lets go of the sack. Tuppenny stops humming.
